.TH BOSWAS-COMPOSITOR 1 "November 2026" "Boswas OS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
boswas-compositor \- the Boswas OS display server and window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boswas-compositor
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas-compositor
is the display server, window manager and effects engine of the Boswas OS
desktop. It draws every window and its frame (title bar, caption buttons,
rounded corners, shadow), animates windows and desktops, routes all input,
and decides which program may see or control what. Applications connect to
it with the standard Wayland protocols; X11 applications run through the
X server it starts for them.
.PP
It is normally started by
.BR boswas-session (1).
Once it accepts clients it starts the shell
.RB ( boswas-shell (1))
as a trusted client and starts it again when it ends (see
.BR "THE SHELL" ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " KIND"
\fBudev\fR: real hardware (DRM/KMS, libinput, libseat); the default on a
virtual terminal.
\fBwinit\fR: a window inside another Wayland or X11 session; the default
there.
\fBheadless\fR: one output rendered in software into memory, for tests.
.TP
.BI \-\-headless\-size " WIDTH" x HEIGHT
Size of the headless output (default 1920x1080).
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " NAME"
Wayland socket name (default: the first free \fBwayland\-\fIN\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read only this configuration file instead of the layers below.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-shell
Do not start the shell.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-input
Accept input-injection commands on trusted control connections. For tests.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-env
Print \fBWAYLAND_DISPLAY\fR and \fBBOSWAS_COMPOSITOR_SOCKET\fR on standard
output once the compositor is ready.
.TP
.BI \-\-send " COMMAND"
Send a command to a running compositor's control socket, print the reply
and exit: \fBversion\fR, \fBlock\fR, \fBreload\-config\fR, \fBoutputs\fR or
\fBcapture\fR. With \fB\-\-control\-socket\fR \fIPATH\fR another
compositor's socket is used.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH STARTING ON REAL HARDWARE
With the \fBudev\fR backend the compositor gets its devices from the login
session's seat (libseat, normally through logind), so it has to be started
from a login session on a virtual terminal: by
.BR greetd (1)
or from a login on a text console. It opens the seat and waits up to 4
seconds for the seat to be enabled before it opens any device. A seat that
is not enabled ends the compositor with a message that names the usual
causes: it was not started from such a session; logind refused to activate
the session because polkit is not running; another display server holds
the seat. What libseat itself reports is in the log (target
\fBlibseat\fR).
.PP
The outputs of one graphics device are driven: the one the firmware
started the machine with, else the first that works. When no graphics
device is there yet (during boot the driver may bind a moment later) the
compositor waits up to 10 seconds for one. Devices may come and go while it
runs: when the device in use goes away (a firmware framebuffer that the
native driver replaces) or none is in use, the next device that appears is
taken, its monitors are set up, and the windows of the session stay.
.PP
A desktop session without a usable monitor keeps running and appears when
one is connected. The login screen instead ends after 5 seconds without an
output, so that the text login is shown.
.PP
Frames are drawn with OpenGL ES where the device has a hardware renderer.
Where OpenGL ES would only run in software (a virtual machine without 3D
acceleration, a firmware framebuffer, the "safe graphics" start entry) or
cannot start at all, they are drawn with pixman into the device's own
buffers instead, which costs a fraction of software OpenGL for a
desktop's flat rectangles; windows then have square corners. The log says
which renderer a device uses ("drawing with"), and
.B BOSWAS_RENDERER
overrides the choice. On devices without a vertical blank (most virtual
graphics adapters, the firmware framebuffer) frames are still shown no
faster than the display's refresh rate.
.SH THE SHELL
The shell is started again at once when it ends. A shell that keeps ending
right after its start (within 30 seconds) is started again after a pause
that doubles each time, from 2 seconds up to a minute: the session and its
applications stay, and the log says when the next try is. A shell that has
run for 30 seconds counts as working again.
.PP
The login screen (the shell command has the argument \fB\-\-greeter\fR) is
different, because nothing else runs on its compositor: after three failed
starts in a row the compositor ends with exit status 4 and one line on
standard error that says how the login screen ended, and
.BR boswas\-greeter
shows the text login. No X server is started for the login screen either.
.SH KEYBOARD
.TS
tab(|);
l lx.
Super|T{
open or close Start
T}
Super+A|T{
quick settings
T}
Super+N|T{
notifications and calendar
T}
Super+R|T{
Run
T}
Super+E|T{
Files
T}
Super+I|T{
Settings
T}
Ctrl+Shift+Esc|T{
Task Manager
T}
Ctrl+Alt+T|T{
Terminal
T}
Super+L|T{
lock the session
T}
Super+1 ... Super+9|T{
start or switch to the taskbar application at that place
T}
Super+Shift+1 ... 9|T{
start another window of it
T}
.sp
Alt+Tab, Alt+Shift+Tab|T{
pick a window, most recently used first
T}
Super+Tab|T{
Task View: every window and the desktops
T}
Alt+F4|T{
close the window
T}
Alt+Space|T{
the window menu: Restore, Move, Size, Minimize, Maximize, Close
T}
F11, Super+F11|T{
